        "content": "# Quick Build: Add Json Validation Script\n\n**Created**: 2026-03-01T00:00:00Z\n**Request**: add a json validation script that's ultra performant\n**Scope**: Small\n\n## Plan\n\n**Reasoning**: This is a small, additive feature touching 2-3 new files in a single system (the existing Bun/TypeScript project). No existing code needs modification beyond optionally adding a script entry to package.json. Risk is low since this is purely additive.\n**Files Affected**: `src/validate-json.ts`, `src/validate-json.test.ts`, `package.json`\n**Approach**: Create a high-performance JSON validation module that avoids schema-library overhead by using a hand-rolled, zero-allocation validation approach. The validator will accept a JSON schema-like definition object and validate input data against it using iterative (not recursive) traversal where possible, pre-compiled type checks, and short-circuit evaluation for early failure. It will leverage TypeScript's type system for compile-time safety while keeping runtime validation lean. A corresponding test file will cover correctness and include a basic performance benchmark. A `validate` script entry will be added to package.json.\n**Estimated Effort**: 1.5 hours\n\n## Tasks\n\n- [ ] **T1**: Create `src/validate-json.ts` with core validation engine -- define a `Schema` type (supporting string, number, boolean, array, object, null, enum, optional fields), implement a `compileSchema` function that pre-processes a schema into an optimized validator closure, and implement a `validateJson` function that runs the compiled validator against input data with short-circuit failure and flat error collection `[complexity:medium]`\n- [ ] **T2**: Add performance-oriented features to the validator -- implement iterative object/array traversal instead of deep recursion, use pre-allocated error arrays, avoid unnecessary string concatenation in the hot path, and add an optional `{fastFail: true}` mode that returns on the first error `[